In this paper, the author gives some properties of the evolution operator when \(\mathrm{char}(K)=2\) and the studies and classifies four types of evolution operators: nilpotent, quasi-constant, ultimately periodic, and plenary train. These classes are defined in the following manner:

\begin{itemize}
\item The evolution operator is \textit{nilpotent of degree \(n\)} provided \(V^n=0\), and \(n\) is minimal with such property.
\item The evolution operator is \textit{quasi-constant of degree \(n\)} provided there is an element \(0\neq e\in A\) such that \(V^n(x)=e\) for every \(x\in A\), and \(n\) is minimal with such property.
\item The evolution operator is \textit{ultimately periodic of pre-period \(n\) and period \(p\)} provided \(V^{n+p}(x)=V^n(x)\) for every \(x\in A\), and \((n,p)\) is minimal with such property (for the colexicographical order).
\item The evolution operator is \textit{plenary train of degree \(n\)} provided there is a polynomial \(T\in K[X]\) of degree \(n\geq 1\) such that \(T(V(x))=0\) for every \(x\in A\), and \(n\) is minimal with such property.
\end{itemize}

